EN = {
 'REPORT.md': {
 0: 'PFC droplets in hydrogel: feasibility and parameter comparison',
 1: '**Result:** published experiments demonstrate optical cavitation of PFC droplets inside hydrogel. This study verifies a conditional finite-PFC model. Target laser threshold, channel formation, collapse jets and impact peaks are **not established**. Missing: the spatial models and measurements listed below.',
 2: 'The earlier ideal-gas control calculates gel deformation and discharge through an existing outlet. Its open-gel peaks are **1.625 m/s outlet speed and 9.143 kPa elastic resistance**. Its sealed control emits zero liquid. These results belong to that outlet model. PFC vaporization and fracture require additional equations.',
 3: '1. Solved models and evidence',
 4: 'Two native COMSOL 6.4 models include **finite real-fluid PFC mass, a vapor core, a surviving liquid shell and intact gel**. Each retains separate inner and outer interfaces. The stationary model solves four phase, mass and traction equations. The transient uses three differential states and four thermodynamic algebraic unknowns. Native Helmholtz EOS functions are checked against independent CoolProp calculations. The transient starts from a supplied post-nucleation seed. Seed formation remains outside the solved model.',
 6: '[S1: embedded-droplet experiments](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jmbbm.2024.106776) establish feasibility in published specimens. Their bubble model includes water vapor and noncondensable gas. These PFC models omit both. Read the [complete derivation](DERIVATION.md) and [source ledger](SOURCES.md).',
 7: '2. Parameters and comparison conditions',
 9: 'Equal stationary stretch compares equilibrium state energies. Required state energy varies between those cases. The viscosity sweep holds retained seed energy fixed within each size. Optical design comparisons must match local incident fluence and absorption law. Mechanical comparisons must match absorbed energy and geometry. Retained seed energy includes storage and ambient work relative to the all-liquid reference. Preparation is lossless here. Measured absorption must also account for pre-seed conduction, viscosity, acoustics and water/coating heating.',
 14: 'PFP requires lower state temperature and energy than PFH here. The 0.6 µm droplet has stronger capillary confinement. Its initial Laplace excess is 360 kPa. The 6 µm droplet has 36 kPa. Their elastic-to-capillary ratios at 20 kPa modulus are 0.111 and 1.111. Smaller droplets therefore face stronger curvature effects despite lower total energy. These temperatures describe finite-mass curved equilibrium states. Optical activation temperatures are **not established**.',
 15: 'The liquid shell survives substantial expansion. At outer stretch 2.1, vapor contains about 20.2% of the smaller PFP mass. The larger case has about 9.2%. A pre-existing gas approximation removes this finite inventory constraint. Reproducing these states requires phase-specific sensible and phase energy.',
 19: 'Higher modulus raises gel resistance and equilibrium state energy. Modulus matters more when capillary confinement is weaker. The 20 kPa, stretch-2.1 state has **30.438 kPa** elastic resistance. The earlier outlet model reaches a separate 9.143 kPa peak. Strength and fracture toughness require independent measurements.',
 22: 'Interface chemistry strongly affects the smaller droplets. Inner liquid/vapor tension sets the core pressure jump. Outer liquid/aqueous tension sets initial compression and gel traction. Use values matching interface, coating, composition and temperature. Shell disappearance requires a new gas/aqueous-gel interface and phase closure.',
 27: 'At 0.01 Pa s, the 0.6 µm case reaches stretch 2.1 in 0.3384 µs. Its peak radial wall speed is 0.9596 m/s. The 6 µm case reaches it in 1.2022 µs. Its peak is 2.7231 m/s. All six cases emit **zero exterior mass**. The small, high-viscosity case still grows at its 1.5 µs cutoff. Its maximum radius is **not established** beyond that window.',
 28: 'At 0.01 Pa s, dimensionless viscous coefficients are 3.333 and 0.333. They correspond to the smaller and larger radii. Higher viscosity slows radial growth in these matched-seed cases. An outlet adds another route for energy and fluid motion. Compare radial motion and outlet flow with their own observables.',
 30: 'All 132 stationary states have finite inventory and positive shell thickness. Phase densities are ordered. Both phase pressure-density slopes are positive. Every state passes the internal core/phase Hessian test at fixed temperature, volume and mass. The minimum bulk-to-capillary curvature ratio is 20.4526. Nucleation accessibility and general shape stability are **not established**.',
 31: '**36** closed-energy states and **94** fixed-temperature states have non-restoring radial slopes. Thermal constraints change the stability result. Halving the perturbation changes closed-energy slopes by at most 3.205e-05 relatively. No sign changes occur. Test branch stability separately from phase-root convergence.',
 33: '7. Optical, fracture and depth screens',
 34: 'Use an illustrative **local incident** fluence of 20 mJ/cm² (200 J/m²). Projected-disk energies are 56.55 pJ and 5.655 nJ for 0.6 and 6 µm diameters. PFP stretch-2.1 state-energy ratios are 0.217 and 0.968. These are required effective cross-section ratios under lossless energy allocation. Effective absorption cross section divided by geometric area can exceed one. Measured absorption and activation thresholds are **not established**. Missing: absorber/coating heating, depth attenuation, nucleation sites and preparation losses. [S7](https://doi.org/10.3390/nano13152238) uses different size, formulation, temperature and exposure history.',
 35: 'A stipulated thermal diffusivity of 1.4 × 10^-7 m²/s and 5 ns pulse gives Fourier numbers 0.00778 and 0.0000778. Heating remains spatially confined during the pulse. Uniform PFC temperature is **not established**. Activation requires spatial heat transfer and a nucleation-rate model. Apply equilibrium closure after a seed forms under compatible conditions.',
 36: 'The slit-cost sweep uses covers 1/5/20/50 µm, half-widths 0.5/1/5 µm and toughnesses 0.1/1/10/100 J/m². These values define hypothetical cracks. A 20 µm cover, 0.5 µm half-width and 1 J/m² toughness costs 20 pJ. Small and large PFP seed energies are 13.50 pJ and 5.857 nJ. Under retained-seed allocation, the smaller case falls below this cost. Fracture impossibility is **not established**. Cooling, interface changes, prestress and other reference-energy changes alter available work. Exceeding the cost also leaves initiation and channel connectivity unresolved.',
 38: 'Finite depth requires a separate geometry check. Center-to-surface distance equals initial radius plus cover. A 3 µm radius under 1 µm cover encounters the initial surface plane at stretch 1.333. This occurs before the 2.1 screen. The infinite-host trajectory then loses its intact-interior interpretation. The CSV records standoff and this gate for every case. Finite-domain convergence remains necessary for other depths.',
 39: '8. Models and measurements still required',
 41: '[The implementation plan](IMPLEMENTATION_HANDOFF.md) lists boundary conditions, tolerances, calibration and held-out comparisons. Begin with one 6 µm PFP droplet and a defined free surface. Retain a 0.6 µm case to test curvature and viscosity. This sequence supports validation. An optimal size is **not established**. Measure absorption, high-rate gel properties, toughness, strength and depth. Couple solid damage to an actual fluid-filled path. A prescribed outlet tests discharge after connection exists.',
 42: '9. Verification and files',
 43: 'Maximum native/reference stationary relative difference is **4.516e-09**. Maximum normalized transient waveform difference is **2.958e-05**. Maximum native normalized energy residual is **4.971e-09**. Independent DOP853/Radau and tighter-step calculations agree. Entropy drift is checked separately from the energy equation. Fresh-process reload and refinement evidence is in `verification/`. The prior independent review corrected seed-energy wording, fracture bounds, depth applicability, branch guards, stability scope and notation.',
 44: 'The [offline reader](index.html) includes a computed-state selector. Download the native [curved-equilibrium model](models/Embedded_PFC_Curved_Equilibrium.mph) and [post-nucleation model](models/Embedded_PFC_PostNucleation_Intact_Gel.mph). The package includes Java/Python sources, parameter and native/reference CSVs, LaTeX, the accepted English PDF and verification records. [README.md](README.md) gives reproduction commands.',
 45: '**Status:** the conditional finite-PFC comparison is complete and numerically verified. Target laser-to-fracture-to-jet validation is **not established**. Missing: specimen-specific spatial models and measured mechanism checks.',
 },
 'DERIVATION.md': {
 0: 'Finite PFC in hydrogel: derivation and validation requirements',
 2: 'Determine the state energy needed to expand a PFC vapor core inside intact hydrogel. Calculate gel load and energy. Identify the additional conditions for exterior ejection and collapse pressure.',
 3: 'This **conditional study** solves finite PFC mass, concentric vapor and liquid phases, real-fluid thermodynamics, separate interfaces, gel elasticity and host viscosity. The transient starts from a **supplied post-nucleation seed**. Its retained energy is calculated relative to an all-liquid reference. Laser heating and seed formation remain unresolved.',
 4: 'Two models are retained. The first solves prescribed-radius curved equilibrium and local stability. The second solves insulated radial expansion from a prescribed seed. It stops at first turning, the declared stretch screen or the time cutoff. Both have zero outlet flow. The effectively infinite, undrained gel has radial inertia. Omitted effects are liquid-shell inertia, thermal gradients, phase-transfer kinetics, damage, water vapor, gas diffusion and acoustic radiation. Inner traction is quasistatic. These assumptions bound every result.',
 5: 'Use spherical coordinates directed outward from the inclusion center. Both radii are positive. Expansion has positive radial speed. Thermodynamic pressures are **absolute**. Resistance and pressure differences are signed excesses. The initial liquid has outer capillary traction and ambient loading. At infinity, displacement and speed vanish; stress approaches minus ambient pressure. PFC mass is closed and exterior mass flux is zero. Both phases have uniform temperature and equal chemical potential. This is a post-activation equilibrium approximation. Nanosecond thermal homogenization is **not established**.',
 6: 'The all-liquid reference starts at room temperature, prescribed radius and Laplace pressure. The seed starts at stretch 1.2 with zero speed and zero accumulated loss. Its liquid pressure exceeds static host traction by 50 kPa. Include seed storage and ambient work. Preparation is lossless here; pre-seed heat, viscous and acoustic losses are omitted. Gel elasticity remains reversible up to the selected screen. Viscosity heats the host. Stretch 2.1 is a **declared stopping screen**. Measured failure strain is **not established**.',
 7: 'Foundations: mass conservation; the first law; Helmholtz EOS [S2](SOURCES.md); continuum interface energy and traction; incompressible neo-Hookean elasticity; Kelvin–Voigt viscosity; spherical inertia [S6, S8](SOURCES.md). The core/shell closure and comparisons are original reduced-model calculations. Physical validation requires activation data, radius histories, damage, exterior mass flux and pressure at a defined sensor.',
 11: 'Gel modulus, viscosity, optical cross section, cover, cohesive strength and toughness are stipulated. They are unmeasured for the target formulation. [The source ledger](SOURCES.md) states the support for interface and EOS inputs.',
 73: '**Symbols before Eq. (E19):** $R,W,D_h$ are radius, speed and accumulated loss (m, m/s, J). Dots denote time derivatives. $T$ is temperature (K); $\\rho_l,\\rho_v,\\rho_h$ are liquid, vapor and host densities (kg/m³); $b$ is core radius (m). $p_l,p_{need}$ are pressures (Pa); $\\eta$ is viscosity (Pa s); $\\gamma_i$ is inner tension (N/m). $p(T,\\rho)$ and $g(T,\\rho)$ are EOS pressure (Pa) and chemical potential (J/kg), with $\\rho$ the density argument (kg/m³). $V,V_b$ are inclusion/core volumes (m³); $m$ is fixed mass (kg). $U_{PFC},E_{gel},E_{surf},E_{amb},K_h,E_{seed}$ are internal, gel, surface, ambient, kinetic and seed energies (J). $u_{l0}$ is initial liquid specific internal energy (J/kg). $\\pi$ is the dimensionless circle constant. $R,b,\\rho_h$ are positive; phases remain admissible.',
 76: '**Symbols before Eq. (E20):** $p_s,U_s,\\tau,E_s$ are positive pressure, speed, time and energy scales (Pa, m/s, s, J). $\\rho_h,a_0,m$ are positive host density, initial radius and PFC mass (kg/m³, m, kg). $\\theta,\\lambda,\\widehat W,\\widehat D$ are dimensionless time, radius, speed and loss; hats label normalized quantities. $t,R,W,D_h$ are dimensional time, radius, speed and loss (s, m, m/s, J). $p_l,p_{need}$ are pressures (Pa); $\\eta$ is viscosity (Pa s). $d/d\\theta$ is differentiation in normalized time. The square root is the positive branch; $\\lambda>0$ permits division.',
 104: '**Symbols before Eq. (E28):** $G,\\eta$ are shear modulus and viscosity (Pa, Pa s); $a_0$ is positive initial radius (m); $\\gamma_o$ is positive outer tension (N/m); $\\rho_h,p_s$ are positive host density and pressure scale (kg/m³, Pa); $\\alpha_h$ is host thermal diffusivity (m²/s); $\\tau_p$ is pulse duration (s); $W,c_h$ are radial speed and positive sound speed (m/s). Vertical bars mean absolute value. The square root takes its positive branch. Every ratio is dimensionless.',
 72: 'These four equations determine temperature, densities and core radius at prescribed stretch. They establish a static phase root. Stability and nucleation-barrier crossing require separate tests. Eq. (E16) gives state energy with zero kinetic energy and loss. Native stationary solves use perturbed initial guesses and solve all four equations.',
 82: 'A positive slope restores radial perturbations. Increased radius receives inward imbalance; decreased radius receives outward imbalance. A negative slope is locally unstable. Recompute phase and energy roots at stretch offsets 0.0001 and 0.00005. Keep both neighboring states admissible. Step 11 tests internal phase/core variations separately. Nonspherical stability is **not established**. The fixed-temperature derivative $k_T$ (Pa per stretch) holds temperature instead of energy. Of 132 sampled states, 36 are non-restoring at closed energy and 94 at fixed temperature.',
 92: 'The hazard is dimensionless and nonnegative. Probability therefore lies between zero and one. Heterogeneous nucleation needs a site or area hazard with matching units. **Unresolved:** absorber/site distribution, kinetic prefactor and temperature history. Activation thresholds are **not established**. Measure activation fractions versus fluence with no-droplet and no-absorber controls.',
 96: 'The energy condition leaves crack initiation, path, aperture and filling unresolved. Fixed-pressure and closed-energy loading give different release rates. Measure toughness and strength independently of modulus. These native controls contain no damage law.',
 102: 'Slit length times width gives projected area. Area times toughness gives work. Compare that work with the assigned retained seed energy. Fracture impossibility is **not established**. Cooling can reduce PFC internal energy; changing topology can release interface energy. An upper bound needs all other reference-energy changes to be nonnegative, or an endpoint-specific available-energy calculation. Neither condition is established here. Flaws, path length, width, prestress and later heat also affect the budget. Passing this screen leaves initiation, connection, aperture and liquid supply unresolved. Cover affects the assumed slit cost; the trajectory still uses an infinite host. Define center-to-surface distance $d=a_0+h$ (m). Define encounter stretch $\\lambda_{surface}=d/a_0$ (dimensionless). At $R\\ge d$, the inclusion intersects the initial surface plane. Smaller standoff still requires finite-domain convergence. A 3 µm radius under 1 µm cover gives encounter stretch 1.333, below 2.1.',
 112: '**Symbols before Eq. (E30):** $T$ is fixed uniform temperature (K); $V$ is fixed inclusion volume (m³); $m$ is fixed PFC mass (kg). Independent coordinates are vapor volume $q=V_b$ (m³) and vapor mass $n=m_v$ (kg), with $0<q<V$ and $0<n<m$. Liquid volume is $V_l=V-q$ (m³); liquid mass is $m_l=m-n$ (kg). Phase densities are $\\rho_l=m_l/V_l$ and $\\rho_v=n/q$ (kg/m³). Core radius is $b=(3q/(4\\pi))^{1/3}$ (m). $F$ is internal Helmholtz energy including the core surface (J); $a_H(T,\\rho)$ is specific Helmholtz energy (J/kg); $\\gamma_i$ is constant inner tension (N/m); $\\pi$ is the circle constant. Outer surface, gel and ambient terms are constant at fixed outer volume.',
 115: '**Symbols before Eq. (E31):** $F$ is internal Helmholtz energy (J); subscripts $n,q$ denote partial derivatives in vapor mass $n$ (kg) and volume $q$ (m³), holding other coordinates fixed. $T$ is fixed temperature (K). $p_l,p_v$ are phase pressures (Pa); $\\rho_l,\\rho_v$ are phase densities (kg/m³); $V_l=V-q$ is liquid volume (m³). $K_l=(\\partial p_l/\\partial\\rho_l)_T$ and $K_v=(\\partial p_v/\\partial\\rho_v)_T$ are positive pressure-density slopes (Pa m³/kg). $\\kappa_l=K_l/V_l$ and $\\kappa_v=K_v/q$ are scaled slopes (Pa/kg). $\\gamma_i$ is inner tension (N/m); $b$ is core radius (m); $\\pi$ is the circle constant. At fixed temperature, $dg=dp/\\rho$. Density derivatives are $\\partial_n\\rho_l=-1/V_l$, $\\partial_n\\rho_v=1/q$, $\\partial_q\\rho_l=\\rho_l/V_l$ and $\\partial_q\\rho_v=-\\rho_v/q$. Mass derivatives have units m^-3. Volume derivatives have units kg/m^6.',
 117: 'The surface derivative uses $dq/db=4\\pi b^2$. Positive pressure-density slopes give $F_{nn}>0$. Mass curvature has units J/kg²; mixed curvature has units Pa/kg; volume curvature has units Pa/m³. For a symmetric Hessian with positive mass curvature, positive definiteness requires a positive Schur complement. **Symbols before Eq. (E32):** $H_{core}$ is that complement (Pa/m³); $F_{qq},F_{nq},F_{nn}$ are volume, mixed and mass curvatures (Pa/m³, Pa/kg, J/kg²). $\\kappa_l,\\kappa_v$ are positive scaled pressure-density slopes (Pa/kg); $\\rho_l,\\rho_v$ are phase densities (kg/m³); $\\gamma_i$ is inner tension (N/m); $b$ is positive core radius (m); $\\pi$ is the dimensionless circle constant. The test holds temperature, total volume and total mass fixed.',
 119: 'Use the positive common denominator for the bulk terms. Squared $\\kappa_l$ and $\\kappa_v$ terms cancel the mixed-curvature terms. The numerator becomes $\\kappa_l\\kappa_v(\\rho_l^2-2\\rho_l\\rho_v+\\rho_v^2)$. Bulk compressibility stabilizes this volume variation; capillarity destabilizes it. All 132 roots have positive mass curvature and Schur complement. The minimum bulk-to-capillary ratio is 20.4526. This establishes a local internal minimum at fixed $(T,V,m)$. Global accessibility, nucleation probability, shape stability and finite-depth dynamics are **not established**.',
 121: 'The audit checks local symbols, units, algebra, branches and references. Automated calculations reconstruct gel-energy and kinetic-power derivatives, phase inventory, traction, chemical equilibrium and seed-energy accounting. Independent waveforms and entropy drift are compared. Stability is tested separately from root existence. These checks verify the declared equations and their implementation.',
 122: '**Unresolved:** optical heat distribution; metastable and heterogeneous nucleation probability; temperature gradients and phase-transfer rates; shell inertia; target high-rate rheology and fracture; surface geometry and crack filling; water vapor and noncondensable gas; nonspherical collapse, breakup and shock/impact pressure. [The implementation plan](IMPLEMENTATION_HANDOFF.md) lists the required measurements. Full target-mechanism validation is **not established**.',
 },
 'IMPLEMENTATION_HANDOFF.md': {
 0: 'Validation plan for an embedded PFC droplet',
 1: 'The native models solve finite-PFC phase equilibrium and radial mechanics. The following gates define specimen-specific prediction. Validate each observable independently.',
 3: 'Record species, purity and size distribution. Label every size as **radius or diameter**. Record shell chemistry/thickness, absorber loading/cross section, gel formulation/hydration, center depth, cover, gel thickness and supports. Record surrounding liquid/air and initial temperature. Measure wavelength, pulse shape, local fluence and focal spot. Define the required output: activation probability, radius history, gel load, channel creation, emitted mass/impulse, collapse jet or sensor pressure.',
 4: 'If the sample is unspecified, start with a 6 µm PFP diameter and one free surface. This matches a published size class and reduces curvature effects. An optimum is **not established**. Retain a 0.6 µm diameter for curvature/viscosity comparison. Current 20 kPa modulus and 0.01 Pa s viscosity are stipulated. For approximately incompressible, small-strain material, shear modulus is approximately Young’s modulus divided by three. Check measurement conventions before conversion.',
 6: 'Resolve heat transfer in absorber, PFC, coating and aqueous gel. Normalize deposited power to measured absorbed energy. Compute local temperature during confined heating. Resolve thermal contact or calibrate the absorber heat-transfer source. Permit heat transfer at PFC/gel interfaces. Initialize the far boundary at measured ambient temperature. Check source integral and sensible heating against a constant-property analytic case. Refine space and time around the absorber and pulse.',
 7: 'Retain metastable liquid before vaporization where physically meaningful. Calibrate heterogeneous nucleation using site geometry and liquid pressure. Measure activation fractions versus fluence. Include no-absorber, no-droplet and water controls. Phase-root convergence does not establish activation. Ambient boiling temperature and conditioned-population thresholds cannot calibrate the target droplet.',
 8: '**Evidence:** source-normalization error below 0.5%; activation probability versus fluence and size; repeatability and uncertainty; local temperature or an independently supported absorber model. Tolerances are proposed project criteria. Three onset fluences provide an initial screen. A reliable probability distribution needs additional trials.',
 10: 'Begin with one nucleated inclusion and intact host. Track PFC liquid and vapor masses separately. Add aqueous-vapor inventory if water vapor forms. Use real-fluid EOS and consistent energy references. Resolve curvature, phase-transfer rate and interfacial enthalpy flux. An enthalpy/internal-energy formulation may already include latent heat; avoid double counting. Add shell inertia and thermal gradients when radius histories require them.',
 11: 'Benchmark early interior growth against the spherical model under its stated assumptions. Add finite-depth axisymmetric gel/fluid domains, finite deformation, dynamic viscoelasticity and moving interfaces. Use axisymmetry when observations support it. Refine the outer domain. Apply measured support or free-surface conditions. Resolve drainage if the undrained timescale condition fails. Choose radius or pressure forcing consistently. Imposing measured radius and arbitrary pressure together overconstrains mechanics.',
 12: '**Evidence:** mass residual below 0.1%; energy residual below 1%; mesh/time/domain refinement changes radius, event time and load by less than 2%. Compare radius histories and displacement fields at **held-out** energies and formulations. Include measurement uncertainty. A provisional 10% agreement target needs more precise observations. Fit absorber energy and viscosity using independent observables.',
 14: 'Measure high-rate strength and fracture energy at relevant hydration and temperature. Use a calibrated cohesive law when the crack path is known. Otherwise validate a tensile damage/fracture law and regularization length. [COMSOL damage documentation](https://doc.comsol.com/6.4/doc/com.comsol.help.sme/sme_ug_solid.07.040.html) and the [cohesive example](https://doc.comsol.com/6.4/doc/com.comsol.help.models.sme.cohesive_zone_debonding/cohesive_zone_debonding.html) describe available implementations. Check installed modules and licenses.',
 15: 'Specify how a crack becomes a fluid domain. A cohesive gap needs aperture, contact, pressure and filling. Diffuse damage needs a permeability/aperture coupling or consistent fluid remeshing. Check mass conservation and pressure work during coupling. Fixed ALE topology requires an explicit treatment when a ligament opens. An initially prescribed channel tests discharge through an existing path.',
 16: '**Evidence:** initiation time/location, crack opening and surface connection, exterior-ejection threshold, fracture work and mesh/regularization convergence. Calibrate one cover thickness and predict another. Include sealed, pre-opened and tougher-gel controls. Cover, width and toughness are separate inputs.',
 18: 'After hydraulic connection, solve liquid supply, aperture and exterior free surface. Integrate mass, kinetic-energy and momentum flux over time. Compare emitted mass and impulse directly. Track composition when the liquid shell disappears. Use the resulting gas/aqueous-gel interface law. Include receiver geometry and breakup for transfer predictions.',
 19: 'Collapse jets require nonspherical, compressible multiphase conservation. Benchmark a water bubble near a known boundary. Then test a gas/gel boundary and the actual PFC/water-vapor mixture with damaged geometry. Resolve acoustic propagation, receiver position and sensor averaging. Use a suitable EOS, radiation/damping boundaries and a shock-capable method. Impact peaks require resolved pressure histories.',
 20: '**Evidence:** high-speed shape and jet images, collapse time, sensor position, bandwidth, impulse and pressure convergence with mesh/time/bandwidth. Compare held-out cases. Singular incompressible pressure extrapolation fails this gate.',
 22: 'Sweep size, species, interface chemistry, fluence/absorption, dynamic modulus/viscosity, cover, strength/toughness, permeability and gas content. Screen one factor at a time, then test dominant interactions. Mechanical comparisons hold **absorbed energy** fixed. Optical comparisons hold **incident fluence and absorption law** fixed. Propagate measured input intervals and report rejected branches and failed solves.',
 23: 'Freeze calibration inputs before held-out tests. Deliver spatial models, source/parameter ledger, source integral, phase/mass/energy accounting, native exports, independent controls and convergence evidence. Separate measured and assumed inputs. Target-mechanism validation requires gates B–E to pass for the target architecture.',
 },
 'SOURCES.md': {
 0: 'Sources and supported claims',
 1: 'Primary and official sources were checked on 2026-10-11. This project’s calculated values are conditional model results. They are separate from literature measurements.',
 3: 'Prior source context is in `sources/prior_interface_evidence.md`. Scholarly links lead to original repositories or DOI pages. EOS coefficients, derivation, models, data and audit records are bundled. The accepted English PDF retains its original source ledger. `PUBLICATION.md` records the current package provenance.',
 },
}

